---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import LayerTrio from '../components/LayerTrio.astro';
import schematic from '../assets/images/schematic.png';
// Text drafted from the proposal (OEC-2026, section 7). TODO: review before launch.
---

<Base title="About" description="The research idea behind Administrative Devices: the three layers of archival structure, the stages of the work, and the archives it is tested on.">
  <article class="container">
    <header class="section head">
      <p class="eyebrow">About the project</p>
      <h1>What digitisation leaves out</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Physical archives are carefully organised information systems. Digitisation keeps the text and drops the
        organisation. Administrative Devices develops methods to recover that organisation and turn it into data.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="section prose">
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>
        Inventories describe boxes or books. These contain folders or sections, which contain documents with their
        own internal structure. At every level there are features that guide the reader: documents grouped by origin,
        period or subject, indexes listing them by topic or date, notes in the margin pointing to related material. We
        call these features <em>administrative devices</em>. They show how information was created, managed and used.
      </p>
      <p>
        Digitisation flattens this hierarchy into a sequence of scans with little more than an inventory number and a
        position in the sequence. Text recognition makes the pages searchable, but the search returns isolated pages.
        It cannot tell you what kinds of documents exist, how many there are, where a document sits in the archive, or
        which resolution an index entry points to. Keyword search over pages without their context is becoming the
        default way of doing historical research, and the source-critical distortions that come with it are spreading.
      </p>
      <figure class="fig">
        <Image
          src={schematic}
          alt="Schematic of a sequence of scans of one inventory number, with documents of different types and lengths running across columns, pages and scans."
          widths={[700, 1100, 1600]}
          sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 720px"
        />
        <figcaption>
          One inventory number as a sequence of scans. Documents of different types and lengths start anywhere on a page
          and continue across columns, pages and scans.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Three layers, three objectives</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        We distinguish three connected dimensions of archival organisation. They are present in most archives, and
        especially regular in administrative archives: the records governments and institutions made to run their
        business.
      </p>
      <LayerTrio showObjective />
    </section>

    <section class="section prose">
      <h2>Research question</h2>
      <blockquote>
        How can we detect, represent, and operationalise the hierarchical structures of digitised historical archives, to
        connect the three levels of structure and transform implicit organisational systems into structured, analysable
        datasets?
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        Recovered structure makes analysis possible that keyword search cannot support. For example, it lets you count
        and compare document types over time, search with filters and facets over the archive's hierarchy, and see
        where each result sits in the archive. In the resolutions of the States General it shows, for instance, that
        far more diplomatic missives were discussed on Mondays to Wednesdays than on Thursdays or Fridays.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose">
      <h2>How we work</h2>
      <p>
        Instead of relying on one model, we combine visual language models with cheaper statistical, clustering and
        sequence methods. A central question is when inexpensive unsupervised analysis is enough and when the costlier
        reasoning of a VLM is needed. The stages are also a dependency order: each early, cheap stage has value on its
        own.
      </p>
      <ol class="stages">
        <li>
          <h3>Stage 1 · Unsupervised exploration</h3>
          <p>
            Characterise and cluster the image streams of an archive, detect formulaic language, and discover recurring
            structures, so that ground truth can be sampled in a balanced way before any annotation exists.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Stage 2 · Supervised extraction</h3>
          <p>
            Recognise document and element templates, segment documents across physical and logical boundaries, detect
            template instances in sequences, identify ordering principles, and find larger groupings such as dossiers
            and sessions.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Stage 3 · Hierarchical integration</h3>
          <p>
            Link everything to finding aids and inventories, with modular components that read and write shared
            formats, record provenance, and can be combined in a notebook.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose">
      <h2>Use cases</h2>
      <p>
        The main use case is the <strong>Resolutions of the States General (1576–1796)</strong>: around 700,000
        resolutions from 220 years of daily meetings, on 537,000 pages in 657 books. They are already transcribed, and
        extensive ground truth exists. Further cases bring different structural challenges:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>the wider States General archive, including the books of correspondence (Nationaal Archief);</li>
        <li>estate records of missing persons, with family trees and forms (NIOD);</li>
        <li>notarial deeds of Amsterdam notaries, and housing cards (Amsterdam City Archives);</li>
        <li>migrant registration cards (Nationaal Archief).</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    padding-bottom: 24px;
  }
  .section.prose,
  .section {
    padding-top: 40px;
    padding-bottom: 40px;
  }
  blockquote {
    margin: 0 0 1.2em;
    padding: 4px 0 4px 20px;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
  }
  .fig {
    margin: 28px 0 0;
  }
  .fig :global(img) {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .fig figcaption {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    margin-top: 8px;
  }
  .stages {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    counter-reset: none;
  }
  .stages li {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--rule);
    padding: 2px 0 2px 20px;
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }
  .stages h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.3em;
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }
</style>
